WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
♪ [music] ♪

00:00:12.949 --> 00:00:14.046
Hey folks!

00:00:14.046 --> 00:00:18.330
My name is Addy Osmani and welcome
to Optimizing for Core Web Vitals.

00:00:18.771 --> 00:00:23.286
Today we're going to talk about 
optimizing user experiences on the web,

00:00:23.286 --> 00:00:27.686
with a case study on 
French luxury fashion house Chloé.

00:00:27.686 --> 00:00:30.906
Chloé have recently been taking 
a fresh look at web performance

00:00:30.906 --> 00:00:33.699
and I'm really excited 
to share their learnings with you.

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:36.140
Now, you may have seen 
Google Search announce

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.626
an upcoming search ranking change recently

00:00:38.626 --> 00:00:41.502
that incorporates 
page experience metrics.

00:00:41.502 --> 00:00:43.930
Now, these metrics 
include the Core Web Vitals

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.427
which, together with a few other signals,

00:00:46.427 --> 00:00:48.338
paint a pretty holistic picture

00:00:48.338 --> 00:00:51.499
about the quality 
of user experiences on a page.

00:00:51.499 --> 00:00:53.729
But, what are the Core Web Vitals

00:00:53.729 --> 00:00:56.144
and how do you go about
optimizing for them?

00:00:56.144 --> 00:01:00.434
Well, Core Web Vitals 
are a set of metrics related to speed,

00:01:00.434 --> 00:01:03.498
responsiveness and visual stability.

00:01:03.498 --> 00:01:08.625
Now these three aspects of user experience
are measured using three metrics.

00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:12.050
So first of all, 
we have Largest Contentful Paint

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.226
which measures loading performance.

00:01:14.226 --> 00:01:18.231
Next up, we have First Input Delay
which measures interactivity.

00:01:18.231 --> 00:01:20.886
And last, we've got 
Cumulative Layout Shift

00:01:20.886 --> 00:01:22.946
which measures layout stability.

00:01:22.946 --> 00:01:27.556
Let's kick things off by talking about
Cumulative Layout Shift or CLS.

00:01:27.918 --> 00:01:31.721
Now, CLS is a pretty important metric
for measuring visual stability

00:01:31.721 --> 00:01:34.196
because it helps quantify all those times

00:01:34.196 --> 00:01:38.827
when we see really surprising shifts
in the content on a page,

00:01:39.319 --> 00:01:42.914
and it helps make sure that the page
is as delightful as possible.

00:01:42.914 --> 00:01:46.624
Have you ever been reading
like an article online

00:01:47.142 --> 00:01:52.431
when, all of a sudden, something
suddenly changes on the page

00:01:53.386 --> 00:01:57.836
and without warning the text moves
and you've lost your place?

00:01:58.304 --> 00:02:00.294
That's literally what happens,

00:02:00.294 --> 00:02:03.279
a giant chicken kicks your content away.

00:02:03.976 --> 00:02:05.856
And he has no regrets, look at him.

00:02:05.856 --> 00:02:08.490
He's basically CLS.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.402
So, what causes poor CLS?

00:02:10.402 --> 00:02:13.927
Well, first of all, we've got 
images without dimensions,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.946
ads, embeds or iFrames
without dimensions,

00:02:17.946 --> 00:02:21.386
dynamically injected content and web fonts

00:02:21.386 --> 00:02:24.832
that might cause a flash
of unstyled content.

00:02:25.242 --> 00:02:29.312
Now, as I mentioned, 
Chloé is a French luxury fashion house,

00:02:29.312 --> 00:02:32.762
and it's become a bit of a go-to brand
not just for like luxury apparel

00:02:32.762 --> 00:02:35.995
but also handbags and fragrances
and things like that.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:40.668
And they have recently been focused
on improving Cumulative Layout Shift

00:02:40.668 --> 00:02:42.570
on all their main pages,

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:45.645
so their Home Page, 
their Product Listings Page

00:02:45.645 --> 00:02:47.492
and their Product Details Page.

00:02:47.992 --> 00:02:50.973
Through a bunch of work 
they've been able to reduce their CLS

00:02:50.973 --> 00:02:55.185
all the way down to zero 
which is about as perfect as you can get.

00:02:55.185 --> 00:02:56.725
So how did they get here?

00:02:56.725 --> 00:02:59.325
This is the before view 
of the Chloé homepage

00:02:59.325 --> 00:03:02.957
where we can observe 
a number of surprising layout shifts

00:03:02.957 --> 00:03:06.957
due to elements on the page 
not following CLS best practices.

00:03:06.957 --> 00:03:10.528
So, let's dive into a few tips
that worked well here.

00:03:10.528 --> 00:03:14.168
First off, always include 
width and height size attributes

00:03:14.168 --> 00:03:16.446
on your images and video elements.

00:03:16.446 --> 00:03:20.773
Alternatively, you can always do things
like reserve the required space

00:03:20.773 --> 00:03:23.025
with CSS aspect ratio boxes,

00:03:23.025 --> 00:03:26.041
but, in general, this approach 
just makes sure that the browser

00:03:26.041 --> 00:03:29.152
can allocate the correct amount
of space in the document

00:03:29.152 --> 00:03:30.615
while the image is loading.

00:03:30.615 --> 00:03:32.447
So, here's a demo of this in action.

00:03:32.447 --> 00:03:36.890
These are some images that don't have
width and height specified

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:38.198
and what you see happening

00:03:38.198 --> 00:03:41.623
is that they're pushing content
in the page all the way down.

00:03:42.049 --> 00:03:45.287
This is something that's reflected
in our tools like Lighthouse.

00:03:45.287 --> 00:03:48.745
I've got a little bit of a clip out here,
you can see the Lighthouse report

00:03:48.745 --> 00:03:52.048
where CLS is in the red 
and not quite where we want it to be.

00:03:52.048 --> 00:03:53.516
So, how do we address this?

00:03:53.516 --> 00:03:56.651
Well, in the early days of the web,

00:03:56.651 --> 00:03:59.531
developers would add 
width and height attributes

00:03:59.531 --> 00:04:02.185
all over the place, they'd add them
to their image tags,

00:04:02.185 --> 00:04:05.939
they make sure that they kept 
enough space allocated on the page

00:04:05.939 --> 00:04:08.917
before browsers 
would start fetching images.

00:04:09.357 --> 00:04:13.179
That was great because it would
minimize re-flow and re-layout.

00:04:13.179 --> 00:04:17.207
Now, when responsive web design
was introduced,

00:04:17.207 --> 00:04:20.807
developers began to omit 
these width and height attributes

00:04:20.807 --> 00:04:24.636
and they started to use CSS 
to resize their images instead.

00:04:24.636 --> 00:04:26.810
One of the downsides to this approach

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.647
is that space could only be
allocated for an image

00:04:29.647 --> 00:04:31.499
once it began to download.

00:04:31.499 --> 00:04:35.466
And, you know, at that point the browser
could determine its dimensions.

00:04:35.466 --> 00:04:38.719
As images load in in that old world,

00:04:38.719 --> 00:04:40.643
you know, the page would re-flow

00:04:40.643 --> 00:04:43.071
as each image appears on the screen,

00:04:43.071 --> 00:04:48.143
and a lot of us got used to our text
suddenly popping down the screen

00:04:48.143 --> 00:04:50.842
which wasn't a very great user experience.

00:04:51.232 --> 00:04:54.311
And this is where aspect ratio comes in.

00:04:54.311 --> 00:04:59.423
So, the aspect ratio of an image 
is the ratio of its width to its height,

00:04:59.423 --> 00:05:03.877
it's pretty common to see this expressed
as two numbers separated by a colon.

00:05:03.877 --> 00:05:08.222
So, for example, 16:9 or 4:3.

00:05:08.222 --> 00:05:11.038
For an X:Y aspect ratio,

00:05:11.038 --> 00:05:15.031
the image is X units wide
and Y units high.

00:05:15.031 --> 00:05:18.586
What that means is that 
if we know one of the dimensions,

00:05:18.586 --> 00:05:20.638
the other one can be determined.

00:05:20.638 --> 00:05:23.230
So for a 16 to 9 aspect ratio,

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:28.577
if dress.jpg has got a 360px height,

00:05:28.577 --> 00:05:33.188
the width is 360 multiplied by 16 over 9

00:05:33.188 --> 00:05:36.305
which gives us 640px.

00:05:36.998 --> 00:05:40.528
I'm not very good at math, 
so hopefully that was helpful.

00:05:40.528 --> 00:05:46.030
Now, modern browsers now set
the default aspect ratio of images

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:49.120
based on an image's 
width and height attributes.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:53.968
So, it's really valuable to set them 
if you want to avoid those layout shifts.

00:05:53.968 --> 00:05:56.080
This is a change in modern browsers,

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:58.768
and it's all thanks 
to the CSS working group.

00:05:58.768 --> 00:06:01.218
They've done some work
that basically allows us

00:06:01.218 --> 00:06:03.424
to just set width and height as normal,

00:06:03.424 --> 00:06:07.565
and this calculates an aspect ratio
based on the width and height attributes

00:06:07.565 --> 00:06:09.297
before the image has loaded.

00:06:09.297 --> 00:06:11.162
So, what we're seeing on screen here,

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:15.612
this is something that's added 
to the default style sheet of all browsers

00:06:15.612 --> 00:06:17.853
and it calculates aspect ratio

00:06:17.853 --> 00:06:20.602
based on the element's 
width and height attributes.

00:06:20.602 --> 00:06:23.591
So, as long as you're providing
width and height,

00:06:23.591 --> 00:06:25.898
the aspect ratio can be calculated

00:06:25.898 --> 00:06:28.841
and everything will hopefully
avoid layout shifts,

00:06:28.841 --> 00:06:31.126
so this is a great best practice
to be following.

00:06:31.126 --> 00:06:34.681
This is also something that works well
with responsive images.

00:06:34.681 --> 00:06:39.854
With <i>srcset</i>, you're generally defining 
images that you want to allow the browser

00:06:39.854 --> 00:06:43.672
to select between, you need to find
sizes for those images.

00:06:43.672 --> 00:06:48.194
To make sure that your image 
width and height attributes can be set,

00:06:48.194 --> 00:06:52.177
just make sure that each image
is using the same aspect ratio.

00:06:52.737 --> 00:06:57.964
And here's that demo once again 
with width and height attributes added.

00:06:57.964 --> 00:07:01.799
Notice that in a modern browser 
you won't see any layout shifts there,

00:07:01.799 --> 00:07:04.547
and the user will get 
a much more pleasant experience.

00:07:04.547 --> 00:07:05.876
So, another reminder,

00:07:05.876 --> 00:07:08.677
set those width and height attributes
as much as you can.

00:07:08.677 --> 00:07:11.216
Here's the impact 
that this change has on Lighthouse.

00:07:11.216 --> 00:07:15.135
As we can see before, 
we went from a CLS of 0.36,

00:07:15.135 --> 00:07:16.259
so we're in the red,

00:07:16.259 --> 00:07:18.568
all the way back to something
that's a little bit better.

00:07:18.568 --> 00:07:21.807
There are one or two other things 
in this page that could have been improved

00:07:21.807 --> 00:07:24.775
but on the whole we've had 
a relatively significant impact

00:07:24.775 --> 00:07:26.267
on reducing layout shift.

00:07:26.267 --> 00:07:27.375
You might be wondering,

00:07:27.375 --> 00:07:30.695
"How can I figure out 
what elements on my page

00:07:30.695 --> 00:07:32.634
are contributing to CLS?"

00:07:33.016 --> 00:07:34.283
We've got you covered.

00:07:34.283 --> 00:07:38.930
So, in Lighthouse we have 
an <i>Avoid large layout shifts</i> audit,

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:43.671
that highlights the top DOM elements 
contributing most of the CLS to the page,

00:07:43.671 --> 00:07:45.143
so check out that audit.

00:07:45.143 --> 00:07:48.098
In dev tools-- 
we also have a good story here--

00:07:48.098 --> 00:07:50.850
so, if you're using 
the dev tools performance panel,

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:54.205
it has an experience section
that can help you detect

00:07:54.205 --> 00:07:55.911
unexpected layout shifts,

00:07:55.911 --> 00:07:59.935
super helpful for finding 
and fixing visual instability issues.

00:07:59.935 --> 00:08:02.492
They get highlighted 
in this experience session

00:08:02.492 --> 00:08:05.996
with some kind of reddish pinkish
layout shift records.

00:08:05.996 --> 00:08:09.421
And if you click on one of those records, 
you'll be able to get more details

00:08:09.421 --> 00:08:13.955
about what was the score, 
where did this element move to and from,

00:08:13.955 --> 00:08:18.198
really great diagnostics to help you
nail down how to fix your CLS.

00:08:18.643 --> 00:08:22.343
So, Chloé's approach to image loading
is that they use a skeleton pattern

00:08:22.343 --> 00:08:26.390
with a Sass-CSS Mixin 
called Bruschetta loading.

00:08:28.265 --> 00:08:29.893
Bruschetta is one of those things

00:08:29.893 --> 00:08:32.711
that are a little bit of a luxury
for me during quarantine.

00:08:32.711 --> 00:08:37.156
They're right up there 
with toilet paper and antibacterial soap,

00:08:37.156 --> 00:08:39.604
but let's stick with bruschetta loading.

00:08:40.388 --> 00:08:42.642
So, this is Chloé's approach
to image loading.

00:08:42.642 --> 00:08:47.362
They have a parent container 
with a color similar to the final image

00:08:47.362 --> 00:08:48.779
that's being loaded.

00:08:48.779 --> 00:08:51.106
Lazy loading strategies like this,

00:08:51.106 --> 00:08:55.951
where you have a little bit of a preview
of what's finally going to be shown,

00:08:55.951 --> 00:08:59.065
are sometimes referred 
to as low quality image placeholders.

00:08:59.065 --> 00:09:03.580
You can use a predominant color
from the final image,

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:06.149
you can use a low resolution image.

00:09:06.149 --> 00:09:09.439
Sometimes people will use 
like a 1 pixel by 1 pixel image

00:09:09.439 --> 00:09:11.422
or something like 10 pixels by 10 pixels,

00:09:11.422 --> 00:09:14.202
something very low resolution
that just gives you a preview

00:09:14.202 --> 00:09:16.269
of what's finally going to be displayed.

00:09:16.679 --> 00:09:20.130
Now lazy loading strategies like this

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.717
which either use a color 
or that kind of placeholder,

00:09:23.867 --> 00:09:26.667
they don't strictly improve
Largest Contentful Paint,

00:09:26.667 --> 00:09:28.707
but they do improve perceived performance,

00:09:28.707 --> 00:09:31.381
so they can still be pretty good
for the user experience.

00:09:31.381 --> 00:09:36.371
Now, what Chloé did here in addition 
to using this skeleton loading approach

00:09:36.371 --> 00:09:38.773
was that they do use responsive images

00:09:38.773 --> 00:09:41.367
and they do make sure 
that they're setting dimensions

00:09:41.367 --> 00:09:44.053
on their images as well to avoid CLS.

00:09:44.053 --> 00:09:46.950
Let's shift the things up,
shift things up.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:48.283
Let's go on to the next tip.

00:09:48.283 --> 00:09:52.411
So, reserve enough space 
for any of your dynamic content,

00:09:52.411 --> 00:09:54.645
things like ads or promos.

00:09:55.361 --> 00:09:57.171
Ideally, you want to make sure

00:09:57.171 --> 00:10:00.357
that you are giving 
any of that content a container

00:10:00.357 --> 00:10:03.630
that it's not going to just, 
you know, bounce out of

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.915
and suddenly cause shifts in the page.

00:10:06.598 --> 00:10:08.963
A related tip to this one is,

00:10:08.963 --> 00:10:13.373
avoid inserting new content
above existing content,

00:10:13.373 --> 00:10:16.431
unless it's in reaction
to a user interaction.

00:10:16.431 --> 00:10:19.755
You want to make sure 
that any layout shifts in your page

00:10:19.755 --> 00:10:22.674
are ones that you are making
a conscious decision around

00:10:22.674 --> 00:10:25.405
and like occur as expected.

00:10:25.405 --> 00:10:26.941
So let's try to visualize this.

00:10:26.941 --> 00:10:29.522
Here's an example of a promo

00:10:29.522 --> 00:10:32.394
where we're dynamically
injected into the page,

00:10:32.394 --> 00:10:34.183
we haven't reserved space

00:10:34.183 --> 00:10:36.779
and it's just pushed everything
all the way down.

00:10:36.779 --> 00:10:39.554
We can see this reflected 
in our Lighthouse call-out

00:10:39.554 --> 00:10:41.058
at the bottom of the screen.

00:10:41.058 --> 00:10:43.528
Now, this is something 
that very typically happens

00:10:43.528 --> 00:10:47.242
with ads, iFrames, promos.

00:10:47.242 --> 00:10:51.099
And these types of assets 
can sometimes be the largest contributors

00:10:51.099 --> 00:10:52.875
to layout shifts on the web.

00:10:52.875 --> 00:10:58.173
Now, many ad networks and publishers
will often support dynamic ad sizes

00:10:58.633 --> 00:11:01.867
and ad sizes that are dynamic

00:11:01.867 --> 00:11:04.214
are something that can 
sometimes increase revenue

00:11:04.214 --> 00:11:06.604
because you're giving people
a lot of flexibility

00:11:06.604 --> 00:11:09.273
around what can go inside there,
your ad slots,

00:11:09.273 --> 00:11:12.822
but it can also be something 
that can potentially negatively impact

00:11:12.822 --> 00:11:15.175
the user experience 
by pushing things down.

00:11:15.175 --> 00:11:17.290
So, that's something 
that you want to avoid.

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:18.669
So, how do we approach this?

00:11:18.669 --> 00:11:20.786
Well, one solution to the problem

00:11:20.786 --> 00:11:24.907
is statically reserving
space for the slot.

00:11:24.907 --> 00:11:28.302
So, you can make sure 
that you're defining a container

00:11:28.302 --> 00:11:33.686
for these ads or embed frames, 
so that regardless of what goes inside,

00:11:33.686 --> 00:11:37.338
you're not shifting the content
of the page around.

00:11:37.338 --> 00:11:41.111
So, here I've got a container 
where I set my width and my height,

00:11:41.111 --> 00:11:44.776
I've set a background color 
but I've also set it to <i>overflow:hidden</i>

00:11:44.776 --> 00:11:46.911
just in case anything dynamic

00:11:46.911 --> 00:11:51.214
is a little bit, you know, 
a little bit taller than the container,

00:11:51.214 --> 00:11:53.954
I still don't want it 
to be able to break out of it.

00:11:53.954 --> 00:11:57.014
Ideally the content fits inside 
of our container

00:11:57.014 --> 00:11:59.583
like our iFrames or whatever else
we might inject in there.

00:11:59.583 --> 00:12:01.036
And what you can do

00:12:01.036 --> 00:12:05.211
if you're somebody 
that has lots of dynamic content

00:12:05.211 --> 00:12:06.974
that gets injected into your page,

00:12:06.974 --> 00:12:09.736
you can take a look at your data,

00:12:09.736 --> 00:12:14.047
look at what are the medians
or the 95th percentile

00:12:14.047 --> 00:12:16.574
widths and heights 
for this dynamic content

00:12:16.574 --> 00:12:18.962
and size your container accordingly.

00:12:18.962 --> 00:12:21.365
That'll just mean 
that you have the best chance

00:12:21.365 --> 00:12:23.994
at still being able to present 
that content to users

00:12:23.994 --> 00:12:27.038
without negatively impacting 
the rest of the user experience.

00:12:27.038 --> 00:12:29.778
So, here's what it looks 
with my pattern in place.

00:12:29.778 --> 00:12:33.346
I've reserved enough space,
and that content pops in,

00:12:33.346 --> 00:12:35.799
but there are no layout shifts in the page.

00:12:35.799 --> 00:12:37.605
So, I'm really happy about that.

00:12:38.035 --> 00:12:41.377
Slightly better is my baseline 
for everything in life at the moment.

00:12:41.377 --> 00:12:44.730
So, this is the Lighthouse 6.0 impact,

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:47.046
we can see that we reduced
our layout shifts

00:12:47.046 --> 00:12:49.682
from 0.24 all the way down to

00:12:50.342 --> 00:12:53.474
about zero, I'm going give myself
about zero, it's in the green.

00:12:53.474 --> 00:12:54.606
So, that's great.

00:12:54.606 --> 00:13:00.546
Let's talk about a production example
of something like this on Chloé.

00:13:00.546 --> 00:13:04.492
Chloé had a promotion banner for shipping

00:13:04.492 --> 00:13:07.415
at the top of their Product Listing Page.

00:13:07.415 --> 00:13:11.585
And you'll see this free standard 
shipping promotion listed in the very top,

00:13:11.585 --> 00:13:13.831
but this wasn't always there.

00:13:14.386 --> 00:13:17.273
There was a time 
when this Product Listing Page

00:13:17.273 --> 00:13:22.935
had a CLS of 0.4 which is really not great
because of two things.

00:13:22.935 --> 00:13:27.482
The first was the way they approached
their dynamic promo banner

00:13:27.482 --> 00:13:30.306
and the way that they approached filters.

00:13:30.306 --> 00:13:32.853
Let's talk about the banner first.

00:13:33.433 --> 00:13:36.901
Now, this banner 
used to be positioned in line

00:13:37.363 --> 00:13:39.457
underneath the main page header.

00:13:39.965 --> 00:13:43.424
And as you can see here, 
it looks kind of harmless.

00:13:43.424 --> 00:13:47.215
But, what's the impact 
of having a dynamically sized banner

00:13:47.215 --> 00:13:48.595
on the user experience?

00:13:48.595 --> 00:13:50.935
Well, we have a video here,
let's take a look.

00:13:50.935 --> 00:13:55.024
As we can see here, 
once the content is fetched

00:13:55.024 --> 00:13:57.047
and rendered for this banner,

00:13:57.047 --> 00:14:00.843
it pushes the content 
for the rest of the page all the way down,

00:14:00.843 --> 00:14:02.532
and that's not very ideal.

00:14:02.532 --> 00:14:05.122
So, how did Chloé go about fixing this?

00:14:05.122 --> 00:14:08.094
Well, they reserved space for this banner.

00:14:08.094 --> 00:14:09.676
The content for this banner

00:14:09.676 --> 00:14:13.102
was also coming 
from a client-side request.

00:14:13.102 --> 00:14:17.765
Therefore, messages were causing
a pretty visual layout shift

00:14:17.765 --> 00:14:20.002
occurring a few seconds into page load.

00:14:20.002 --> 00:14:23.456
Now, they moved this API call
straight to the server

00:14:23.456 --> 00:14:26.794
and they made sure to reserve
enough space for the banner

00:14:26.794 --> 00:14:28.427
with a simple height setting.

00:14:28.427 --> 00:14:32.139
As a part of this work they moved 
the position of the banner up a little bit

00:14:32.139 --> 00:14:33.389
but altogether,

00:14:33.389 --> 00:14:36.603
like moving more work to the server,
always a good idea,

00:14:37.205 --> 00:14:39.647
and just making sure 
that they're reserving space,

00:14:39.647 --> 00:14:41.582
these things made a bit of a difference.

00:14:41.582 --> 00:14:43.637
So, here's the after view.

00:14:44.317 --> 00:14:47.483
Here we can see the impact 
to their Product Listing Page.

00:14:47.483 --> 00:14:52.456
After these changes have been made,
it's a lot less shifty.

00:14:52.456 --> 00:14:54.188
So, I'm happy about that.

00:14:54.188 --> 00:14:56.780
So, we talked about their promo banner.

00:14:56.780 --> 00:15:01.303
The other big CLS issue 
for product listing pages

00:15:01.303 --> 00:15:05.261
was that Chloé had a filters widget
for filtering products.

00:15:05.261 --> 00:15:09.901
Now, this would rehydrate 
to become dynamic once it booted up

00:15:09.901 --> 00:15:15.685
and so, on the client, 
it was pending XHR calls for data,

00:15:15.685 --> 00:15:19.120
was waiting on session state
based on filter choices

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:22.742
in order to be able to finally 
render this thing on the screen.

00:15:23.459 --> 00:15:26.067
So, this is what 
this basically looked like.

00:15:26.067 --> 00:15:29.334
We wait for kind 
of content to be sent down

00:15:29.334 --> 00:15:31.319
for the filter widget, 
we wait for hydration

00:15:31.319 --> 00:15:34.260
and it would still push content 
on the screen all the way down.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:38.025
What they ended up doing here
was that they adapted this widget

00:15:38.025 --> 00:15:41.681
to contain more of the information 
needed to render the filter widget,

00:15:41.681 --> 00:15:45.092
server-side they'd rendered it
with better defaults.

00:15:45.092 --> 00:15:47.294
This helped avoid those layout shifts.

00:15:47.294 --> 00:15:50.877
And I just wanted 
to give a call out here to the--

00:15:51.348 --> 00:15:54.874
right of the screen we can see 
the Web Vitals Chrome Extension.

00:15:55.319 --> 00:15:58.923
This gives you a real-time view
of all of your vitals metrics

00:15:58.923 --> 00:16:02.525
and can just be helpful 
as you are building your sites locally,

00:16:02.525 --> 00:16:05.371
or you're just browsing the web
and want to get a sense

00:16:05.371 --> 00:16:09.080
for the performance of different sites
that you check out on the regular.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:15.461
And here's what things look like 
after their rehydration fix for filters.

00:16:15.987 --> 00:16:19.667
As you can see, 
CLS reduced by a decent amount,

00:16:19.667 --> 00:16:21.409
looking at the before and after,

00:16:21.409 --> 00:16:23.451
and it was just another case of like,

00:16:23.451 --> 00:16:25.821
pay attention 
to the little things in your pages

00:16:25.821 --> 00:16:29.418
that might be in aggregate 
causing lots of things to be pushed down.

00:16:29.418 --> 00:16:32.324
Every little CLS fix helps.

00:16:33.265 --> 00:16:36.633
And here's the overall impact 
of these changes on Desktop.

00:16:36.633 --> 00:16:40.576
We can see that the above-the-fold content 
is relatively stable

00:16:40.576 --> 00:16:43.901
and offers a much better 
user experience on the whole.

00:16:43.901 --> 00:16:46.745
And this is also reflected in Lighthouse.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:50.162
Work on Lighthouse, 
got to give Lighthouse a shout out.

00:16:50.162 --> 00:16:52.970
As we can see here 
Cumulative Layout Shift is in the green,

00:16:52.970 --> 00:16:55.564
we've hit zero, 
so it's in a really solid place.

00:16:55.564 --> 00:17:00.089
To improve CLS, Chloé acted 
on a number of different elements.

00:17:00.089 --> 00:17:01.980
It wasn't just one thing.

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:06.008
They reserved space for the Promo Content
in terms of its ratio.

00:17:06.008 --> 00:17:09.145
They made sure to set width 
and height dimensions on their images

00:17:09.145 --> 00:17:13.724
and they adopted a skeleton pattern 
which improved perceived performance.

00:17:14.234 --> 00:17:17.834
They reserved space 
for their Promo Banners requests

00:17:17.834 --> 00:17:19.357
before receiving messages.

00:17:19.357 --> 00:17:22.320
And they also reserved space 
for the filters dynamic component

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:24.505
as well as making 
a few other optimizations

00:17:24.505 --> 00:17:26.768
to just help with rendering.

00:17:27.465 --> 00:17:29.919
So, on the whole 
it was definitely worth it.

00:17:30.585 --> 00:17:33.983
Alright. So, I have 
a big surprise for you.

00:17:34.703 --> 00:17:37.011
We've got more metrics to talk about.

00:17:38.531 --> 00:17:40.422
Put a lot of work into this slide.

00:17:40.908 --> 00:17:44.608
Historically, it's been 
a bit of a challenge for web developers

00:17:44.608 --> 00:17:48.689
to measure just how quickly 
the main content of the webpage loads

00:17:48.689 --> 00:17:50.108
and is visible to users.

00:17:50.673 --> 00:17:53.757
Thankfully, we now have metrics
like Largest Contentful Paint

00:17:53.757 --> 00:17:58.151
that are able to report the render time
of the largest content element

00:17:58.151 --> 00:18:00.249
that's visible within the viewport.

00:18:00.249 --> 00:18:03.397
Now, you might be wondering
what causes a poor LCP.

00:18:03.397 --> 00:18:05.069
Well, there are lots of things.

00:18:05.069 --> 00:18:07.289
Slow server response times are a big one.

00:18:07.289 --> 00:18:10.182
This could be 
your back-end infrastructure,

00:18:10.182 --> 00:18:13.482
it could be unoptimized database queries,

00:18:13.482 --> 00:18:17.373
API responses that are just 
taking a while to resolve.

00:18:17.373 --> 00:18:19.984
It could be render blocking
JavaScript and CSS.

00:18:19.984 --> 00:18:23.246
Slow resource times are another big one.

00:18:23.246 --> 00:18:27.286
You could have unoptimized images
slowing down your LCP.

00:18:27.286 --> 00:18:29.547
And then, there's client-side rendering.

00:18:29.547 --> 00:18:31.542
There's a whole class of problems

00:18:31.542 --> 00:18:35.046
where those of us who love
working in JavaScript

00:18:35.046 --> 00:18:38.106
and using modern libraries 
and frameworks and bundlers,

00:18:38.106 --> 00:18:43.543
can sometimes get into a place 
where we have our requests

00:18:43.543 --> 00:18:46.758
for assets like images, 
in particular hero images,

00:18:47.468 --> 00:18:49.522
behind JavaScript fetches.

00:18:49.522 --> 00:18:52.312
The browser, first of all, 
has to fetch your JavaScript.

00:18:52.312 --> 00:18:56.382
Then it has to parse and process 
that JavaScript to fetch your image.

00:18:56.382 --> 00:18:59.862
And that whole process can take so long

00:18:59.862 --> 00:19:02.791
that you delay showing 
meaningful content to your user.

00:19:02.791 --> 00:19:05.579
So, it's just things like that 
you should keep an eye on.

00:19:05.579 --> 00:19:08.663
There are plenty of tools 
that can help diagnose these issues.

00:19:08.663 --> 00:19:13.502
So let's take a look at some real-world
production challenges around LCP

00:19:13.502 --> 00:19:14.926
and how to work around them.

00:19:14.926 --> 00:19:19.270
Chloé started off with an LCP
of about 10 or 11 seconds.

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:23.627
In this view here, we can see 
that their primary hero image content

00:19:23.627 --> 00:19:28.248
wasn't getting fetched and rendered 
until about 11 seconds into our trace.

00:19:28.248 --> 00:19:30.054
Their home page suffers from,

00:19:30.054 --> 00:19:32.639
in this case, it suffered from 
a few different things.

00:19:32.639 --> 00:19:38.192
It had heavy full-screen image downloads,
poorly optimized images,

00:19:38.192 --> 00:19:41.480
some images that were requested late
in the network chain.

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:44.446
And these are very common issues,
there's nothing here

00:19:44.446 --> 00:19:46.840
that they're doing crazy wrong,

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:48.470
it's just very common issues.

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:52.998
And it's useful to be aware 
of some of the things that impacts LCP.

00:19:52.998 --> 00:19:56.527
So, things that impact LCP
are image elements,

00:19:57.083 --> 00:20:00.162
image elements that be inside
of an SVG element,

00:20:00.162 --> 00:20:03.618
video elements, block-level elements
containing text nodes.

00:20:04.520 --> 00:20:07.024
Let's talk about images first

00:20:07.024 --> 00:20:10.977
because they're pretty often
a cause for poor LCP.

00:20:11.474 --> 00:20:16.515
For many sites, images 
are the largest element in view

00:20:16.515 --> 00:20:18.387
when the page is finished loading,

00:20:18.387 --> 00:20:22.791
especially as UX patterns
have shifted towards us

00:20:22.791 --> 00:20:25.548
using more hero images in our pages.

00:20:25.548 --> 00:20:29.524
So, it's very, very important 
to optimize our images,

00:20:29.524 --> 00:20:32.751
especially anything that's visible
within the initial viewport.

00:20:32.751 --> 00:20:35.488
Now, there are a few techniques
that you can use here.

00:20:36.026 --> 00:20:40.042
You can consider not having
an image in the first place,

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:42.852
if it's not that relevant,
maybe you remove it.

00:20:43.848 --> 00:20:45.274
Compress those images,

00:20:45.274 --> 00:20:49.065
use, you know-- there are plenty
of image optimization tools out there,

00:20:49.065 --> 00:20:50.253
compress your images.

00:20:50.253 --> 00:20:53.417
Maybe consider converting them 
to more efficient modern formats,

00:20:53.417 --> 00:20:55.037
use responsive images.

00:20:55.037 --> 00:20:57.827
And you can also consider
using an image CDN.

00:20:58.467 --> 00:21:02.861
I'm seeing an increasing number
of sites leveraging image CDNs

00:21:02.861 --> 00:21:07.785
just to help them 
get control over an ability

00:21:07.785 --> 00:21:11.159
to just tweak parameters
in a URL for an image

00:21:11.159 --> 00:21:15.449
and change what format gets served down
or what quality you have.

00:21:16.331 --> 00:21:19.263
And it's just-- using an image CDN
can be a really good way

00:21:19.263 --> 00:21:21.753
of staying on top of modern best practices

00:21:21.753 --> 00:21:26.649
because-- even us that are, 
you know, web enthusiasts,

00:21:26.649 --> 00:21:28.887
sometimes have a hard time
staying on top

00:21:28.887 --> 00:21:31.662
of everything happening 
in the image optimization world.

00:21:31.662 --> 00:21:33.035
Now, you might be wondering,

00:21:33.035 --> 00:21:38.039
how can I identify the element
that is my LCP?

00:21:39.173 --> 00:21:41.425
Thankfully, we've got some solutions here.

00:21:41.425 --> 00:21:43.569
In dev tools, in the performance panel,

00:21:43.569 --> 00:21:47.496
if you record a trace 
and you go to timings,

00:21:47.496 --> 00:21:49.683
you should find a record for LCP.

00:21:50.251 --> 00:21:54.122
Click on that record and you'll get
the summary pane showing up

00:21:54.122 --> 00:21:56.463
that includes things 
like the size of the image

00:21:56.463 --> 00:21:58.751
and more importantly the related node.

00:21:58.751 --> 00:22:02.685
So, if you hover over that related node,
it will highlight what in your page

00:22:02.685 --> 00:22:04.193
was considered LCP.

00:22:04.193 --> 00:22:06.218
I personally find this really valuable

00:22:06.788 --> 00:22:11.249
as kind of a stepping stone to where
should I be spending my time optimizing.

00:22:11.249 --> 00:22:14.304
So, check that out 
if you use the performance panel.

00:22:14.304 --> 00:22:17.553
This is also something 
that we try to capture in Lighthouse.

00:22:17.553 --> 00:22:21.959
Lighthouse has got 
a Largest Contentful Paint element audit,

00:22:21.959 --> 00:22:25.545
and we try to highlight 
what element was responsible here too.

00:22:25.545 --> 00:22:28.381
So, if you use Lighthouse, check that out.

00:22:28.381 --> 00:22:29.866
So, back to Chloé.

00:22:29.866 --> 00:22:34.778
Chloé discovered that they were delivering
very high resolution images

00:22:34.778 --> 00:22:38.066
even very high resolution
for retina screens.

00:22:38.066 --> 00:22:40.718
Because there is a bit of a cutoff point

00:22:40.718 --> 00:22:46.020
where, if you're serving kind of 
two-by, three-by images,

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:50.214
the human eye 
is not going to be able to perceive

00:22:50.214 --> 00:22:52.332
large amounts of difference there.

00:22:52.332 --> 00:22:54.488
And there are kind of, you know,

00:22:54.488 --> 00:22:57.399
you have diminishing values 
that you get out of just serving

00:22:57.399 --> 00:22:59.862
very, very, very, high resolution images.

00:22:59.862 --> 00:23:03.095
Now, in this case, we're in dev tools-- 
we're in the elements panel,

00:23:03.095 --> 00:23:04.718
we're looking at a specific image,

00:23:04.718 --> 00:23:09.307
and what we see is that the maximum
width of images being served down

00:23:09.307 --> 00:23:14.535
is 1920 pixels, it's pretty large.

00:23:14.535 --> 00:23:17.608
So, one of the things 
that Chloé actually decided to do

00:23:17.608 --> 00:23:19.231
was change things up here.

00:23:19.231 --> 00:23:22.733
They resized their images 
to not be more than two times

00:23:22.733 --> 00:23:24.473
the image viewport size.

00:23:24.473 --> 00:23:29.881
So, they removed srcset sizes
over 828 width

00:23:29.881 --> 00:23:32.985
to keep an image maximum size 
that they were comfortable with

00:23:32.985 --> 00:23:36.937
and that actually ended up being 
pretty fine on retina devices as well.

00:23:36.937 --> 00:23:41.579
There's this nice trade-off 
of how do we deliver rich imagery

00:23:41.579 --> 00:23:44.248
without negatively impacting
the user experience.

00:23:45.118 --> 00:23:50.974
Now, by doing this work 
on an iPhone X or a Pixel 2 XL,

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:56.108
that was previously seeing 
anywhere up to 245 KB

00:23:56.108 --> 00:23:57.898
worth of image bytes being downloaded,

00:23:57.898 --> 00:24:00.871
they were able to reduce it down to 125.

00:24:01.383 --> 00:24:07.644
That's huge, that's like a 51% decrease
in image bytes being served down

00:24:07.644 --> 00:24:09.449
with no noticeable difference.

00:24:09.449 --> 00:24:12.107
So, optimize your images, people.

00:24:12.107 --> 00:24:13.984
The next thing 
we're going to talk about

00:24:13.984 --> 00:24:17.948
is some of the other image optimizations
that they performed.

00:24:17.948 --> 00:24:22.156
So, on the Product Listing Page,
Chloé used image lazy loading

00:24:22.156 --> 00:24:25.192
which, you know, 
is a relatively popular pattern.

00:24:25.192 --> 00:24:28.762
What they discovered was that 
there were four primary images

00:24:28.762 --> 00:24:30.693
being loaded above the fold.

00:24:30.693 --> 00:24:33.518
However, there was one off-screen image

00:24:33.518 --> 00:24:36.954
that seemed to be tripping up
their lazy loading heuristics

00:24:36.954 --> 00:24:38.668
and was still being fetched.

00:24:38.668 --> 00:24:43.522
This particular image 
happened to be 248 KB in size,

00:24:43.522 --> 00:24:45.513
about 200 plus KB in size,

00:24:45.513 --> 00:24:49.665
and this was negatively impacting
the user experience.

00:24:49.665 --> 00:24:51.529
So, they wanted to try improving this.

00:24:52.749 --> 00:24:55.775
On the whole, there are
a number of things Chloé did.

00:24:56.608 --> 00:25:01.346
They were able to bring down 
their above-the-fold image download size

00:25:01.346 --> 00:25:04.026
all the way to 14.5 KB.

00:25:04.026 --> 00:25:07.764
They were able to tune 
their lazy loading heuristics

00:25:07.764 --> 00:25:10.870
so that off-screen images, 
like the one I was just talking about,

00:25:10.870 --> 00:25:12.423
were no longer a problem.

00:25:12.423 --> 00:25:17.482
They adopted an image CDN, 
they adopted WebP by defaults,

00:25:17.482 --> 00:25:20.460
improved their image resizing strategy.

00:25:20.924 --> 00:25:23.576
And the results of this, 
outside of just having

00:25:23.576 --> 00:25:26.689
a nice Lighthouse report 
with lots of greens

00:25:26.689 --> 00:25:31.814
is that each product page 
now weighs 57% less than it did before

00:25:32.179 --> 00:25:34.651
which is a really nice outcome to have

00:25:34.651 --> 00:25:36.805
as a result of optimizing your images.

00:25:36.805 --> 00:25:41.440
Taking a step back, 
here's what the homepage LCP looked like

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:42.890
after these changes.

00:25:42.890 --> 00:25:46.581
We can see that again,
previously those hero images

00:25:46.581 --> 00:25:49.817
were not rendering in 
until about 11 seconds in.

00:25:50.136 --> 00:25:54.071
Now LCP happens 
at about 4 seconds into the process,

00:25:54.071 --> 00:25:56.555
and it's complete 
just a few seconds later.

00:25:56.555 --> 00:26:01.121
The request time for our LCP related node
for our hero images

00:26:01.121 --> 00:26:03.352
is about 1.3 seconds in,

00:26:03.352 --> 00:26:05.619
so on the whole, this is really great.

00:26:05.619 --> 00:26:09.508
There's still work they could do here 
but on the whole this is fantastic to see.

00:26:09.508 --> 00:26:12.265
So let's switch things up to our next tip.

00:26:13.106 --> 00:26:16.689
Defer any non-critical JavaScript and CSS

00:26:16.689 --> 00:26:20.313
to speed up loading 
the main content of your page.

00:26:20.313 --> 00:26:22.524
Now, this is guidance that is not new,

00:26:22.524 --> 00:26:24.247
it's been around for a few years,

00:26:24.247 --> 00:26:26.543
but for anyone that's not familiar
with this guidance,

00:26:26.543 --> 00:26:29.331
I'll give you a very quick recap of it.

00:26:29.331 --> 00:26:32.678
Now, before a browser 
can render any content,

00:26:32.678 --> 00:26:35.789
it needs to parse HTML markup
into a DOM tree.

00:26:35.789 --> 00:26:40.627
The parser needs to pause 
if it encounters any external style sheets

00:26:40.627 --> 00:26:42.757
or synchronous script.

00:26:42.757 --> 00:26:48.793
And scripts and style sheets can both
be render blocking resources

00:26:48.793 --> 00:26:52.527
which can delay 
your First Contentful Paint,

00:26:52.527 --> 00:26:55.469
consequently, your Largest
Contentful Paint as well.

00:26:55.469 --> 00:26:59.540
So what we tell people to do 
is defer any of your non-critical scripts

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:01.953
and style sheets to speed upload.

00:27:01.953 --> 00:27:05.742
So, let's take a look once again 
at the Product Listing Page for Chloé.

00:27:05.742 --> 00:27:09.691
As we can see, this is a trace independent
of their image optimizations

00:27:09.691 --> 00:27:12.808
and as we can see here,
Lighthouse highlights

00:27:12.808 --> 00:27:15.769
that there are a few 
render-blocking style sheets

00:27:15.769 --> 00:27:19.690
that are delaying early paints
on the Product Listing Page.

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:21.696
Now, this is kind of manifested

00:27:21.696 --> 00:27:25.239
in terms of how much white
we're seeing in our filmstrip.

00:27:25.239 --> 00:27:30.032
One approach to addressing this problem
is by inlining your critical CSS

00:27:30.032 --> 00:27:32.899
and deferring the load
of non-critical styles.

00:27:32.899 --> 00:27:35.919
We often call this technique critical CSS.

00:27:35.919 --> 00:27:40.124
Critical CSS is all about extracting CSS

00:27:40.124 --> 00:27:42.182
for above-the-fold content,

00:27:42.182 --> 00:27:45.809
ideally across a number 
of different breakpoints

00:27:45.809 --> 00:27:47.909
and making sure

00:27:47.909 --> 00:27:50.652
that you can render 
the above-the-fold content

00:27:50.652 --> 00:27:53.849
as quickly as possible 
in the first few RTTs

00:27:53.849 --> 00:27:57.568
and deferring the load of the rest
of your style sheets for the page,

00:27:57.568 --> 00:28:01.468
you know for things below the fold,
as soon as possible otherwise.

00:28:02.082 --> 00:28:03.473
So, how did Chloé do this?

00:28:03.473 --> 00:28:05.344
Well, they built some tooling.

00:28:06.079 --> 00:28:09.982
They implemented critical CSS
in their Sass build process

00:28:09.982 --> 00:28:12.037
and they constructed a syntax

00:28:12.037 --> 00:28:16.039
allowing their developers 
to specify for each widget

00:28:16.039 --> 00:28:20.164
what part of the CSS code 
goes into their critical CSS.

00:28:20.164 --> 00:28:22.742
This is highlighted 
using the critical keywords

00:28:22.742 --> 00:28:24.546
you see on the screen right now.

00:28:24.546 --> 00:28:26.048
Now, at build time,

00:28:26.048 --> 00:28:28.686
they're able to build 
both the critical CSS

00:28:28.686 --> 00:28:30.704
and the non-critical CSS,

00:28:30.704 --> 00:28:33.577
so that every single build
is consistent with both.

00:28:34.317 --> 00:28:37.151
There are many ways 
that you can approach critical CSS.

00:28:37.650 --> 00:28:41.218
I've contributed to some tooling
on this topic in the past,

00:28:41.626 --> 00:28:45.717
and you can automate it,
you can go very custom.

00:28:45.717 --> 00:28:49.337
I see some teams 
that will just have a critical <i>.css</i> file

00:28:49.337 --> 00:28:51.624
that they manually curate.

00:28:51.624 --> 00:28:54.280
And regardless 
of the approach that you take,

00:28:54.280 --> 00:28:56.088
what's key is just making sure

00:28:56.088 --> 00:29:00.570
that you're delivering important content
to the user as quickly as possible.

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:02.202
So, we talked about the need

00:29:02.202 --> 00:29:05.547
for loading in the other
style sheets for the page.

00:29:05.547 --> 00:29:11.720
What Chloé do is, their non-critical 
CSS style sheets are stored in an array

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:14.456
so they point to references
to them on their servers,

00:29:14.456 --> 00:29:17.524
and that's injected 
with a deferred script,

00:29:17.524 --> 00:29:20.222
so that it's hopefully not render-blocking

00:29:20.222 --> 00:29:23.576
but is still loaded 
with a relatively high priority

00:29:23.576 --> 00:29:26.672
that isn't going to interfere
with the HTML parser.

00:29:27.368 --> 00:29:31.171
So, what was the impact 
of optimizing their critical CSS?

00:29:31.171 --> 00:29:33.095
Well, the answer is pretty large.

00:29:33.095 --> 00:29:36.578
They were able to bring down
their First Contentful Paint

00:29:36.578 --> 00:29:39.224
from 2.1 seconds to about 1.1,

00:29:39.809 --> 00:29:44.150
and their LCP 
from 2.9 seconds to about 1.5.

00:29:45.001 --> 00:29:47.235
Now, this is really great work.

00:29:47.235 --> 00:29:50.813
Optimizing your critical CSS 
can be a bit of a time investment

00:29:50.813 --> 00:29:52.800
but is something 
that can just make sure

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:55.658
that your page is getting styled
as soon as possible.

00:29:56.299 --> 00:29:57.992
So, let's talk about another tip.

00:29:58.600 --> 00:30:01.328
I mentioned slow server response times

00:30:01.328 --> 00:30:03.976
when we were discussing
what impacts LCP.

00:30:04.560 --> 00:30:09.504
The longer that it takes a browser
to receive content from the server,

00:30:09.999 --> 00:30:13.507
the longer that it takes 
to render anything on the screen.

00:30:14.109 --> 00:30:17.097
The faster a server can respond,

00:30:17.097 --> 00:30:20.085
that's going to improve 
every single page-load metric,

00:30:20.085 --> 00:30:21.765
including LCP.

00:30:21.765 --> 00:30:23.032
So you might be wondering

00:30:23.032 --> 00:30:25.448
how can I tell if I have 
a slow server response time.

00:30:25.448 --> 00:30:26.720
Lighthouse has you covered.

00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:31.116
In Lighthouse we have an audit called
<i>Reduce initial server response time</i>.

00:30:31.116 --> 00:30:33.055
And if you see this,

00:30:33.581 --> 00:30:36.988
it's a good hint to spend more time
diagnosing the problem

00:30:36.988 --> 00:30:39.074
and causes of the problem.

00:30:39.074 --> 00:30:42.659
As I mentioned earlier, it can be 
plenty of things on your back-end.

00:30:42.659 --> 00:30:46.433
And we're trying to optimize
our server response times.

00:30:46.433 --> 00:30:51.237
There's plenty that we can do 
in terms of optimizing, you know,

00:30:53.077 --> 00:30:56.826
our DNS, our pre-connects 
all of those types of things

00:30:56.826 --> 00:31:00.475
but there are also things that we can do
to optimize loading priority.

00:31:01.302 --> 00:31:04.686
This is where techniques
like [link rel] Preload and Server Push

00:31:04.686 --> 00:31:06.369
can come into play.

00:31:06.369 --> 00:31:09.827
Now, if you're new to server push, 
I'll give you a quick summary of it.

00:31:09.827 --> 00:31:14.799
To improve latency, HTTP/2 introduced
this idea of Server Push,

00:31:15.365 --> 00:31:19.715
which basically allows a server 
to push resources to the browser

00:31:19.715 --> 00:31:22.202
before they're explicitly requested.

00:31:22.737 --> 00:31:25.659
Now, you and I, as developers, we can--

00:31:25.659 --> 00:31:28.573
as well as anyone else watching,
you're all awesome too--

00:31:28.573 --> 00:31:33.267
we often know what the most important
resources are on a page,

00:31:33.267 --> 00:31:39.046
and so we can start pushing those 
as soon as, you know, things respond

00:31:39.046 --> 00:31:41.096
with the initial requests.

00:31:41.096 --> 00:31:45.635
This allows the server to fully utilize
what's otherwise an idle network

00:31:45.635 --> 00:31:47.834
to improve page load times.

00:31:48.369 --> 00:31:52.259
Now Server Push is not without its nuance.

00:31:53.815 --> 00:31:56.902
This is one of those optimizations 
where you need to be careful.

00:31:57.410 --> 00:32:00.531
It's possible to over push.

00:32:00.531 --> 00:32:04.298
So, Server Push is not HTTP cache aware,

00:32:04.298 --> 00:32:07.496
so I could push something 
for a particular page,

00:32:07.496 --> 00:32:09.983
the user could come back
to another related page,

00:32:09.983 --> 00:32:13.272
and the server would push 
those exact same resources again.

00:32:13.802 --> 00:32:19.518
The way to avoid that is by either
using cookies or a serviceworker

00:32:19.518 --> 00:32:24.139
to avoid those refetches 
for those types of resources

00:32:24.139 --> 00:32:26.038
and track what's in the cache,

00:32:26.038 --> 00:32:28.185
but it does involve 
a little bit more work.

00:32:28.185 --> 00:32:31.405
In general, Server Push is an optimization
that can have a big impact

00:32:31.405 --> 00:32:33.644
but just be aware of some of that nuance.

00:32:33.644 --> 00:32:37.073
It's not quite as simple 
as just like turning it on sometimes.

00:32:37.073 --> 00:32:41.557
Now, Chloé used automatic Server Push,

00:32:41.557 --> 00:32:44.091
which is an implementation
provided by Akamai.

00:32:44.091 --> 00:32:47.032
It uses data to decide, you know,

00:32:47.032 --> 00:32:50.140
when to push critical CSS,
fonts and script.

00:32:50.140 --> 00:32:54.180
And if you're manually 
using Server Push yourself,

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:57.496
you might end up looking at syntax
that looks a little bit like this.

00:32:57.496 --> 00:33:00.810
What we see here is the link HTTP header.

00:33:01.641 --> 00:33:05.081
This is actually the preload
resource hint in action,

00:33:05.081 --> 00:33:08.662
and it's a separate 
but distinct optimization

00:33:08.662 --> 00:33:09.867
from Server Push.

00:33:09.867 --> 00:33:15.340
But in reality, most HTTP/2 implementations
will push an asset

00:33:15.953 --> 00:33:20.073
that's specified in a link header 
containing a preload resource hint

00:33:20.073 --> 00:33:25.686
so you can use the syntax in order 
to enable server pushes for a page.

00:33:26.137 --> 00:33:28.301
So, what was the impact
of this optimization?

00:33:28.301 --> 00:33:31.784
Without Server Push, 
Chloé were finding in their lab tests

00:33:31.784 --> 00:33:35.274
that LCP was closer to four seconds.

00:33:35.274 --> 00:33:38.784
But with it, it was closer to 2.5 seconds

00:33:38.784 --> 00:33:41.207
which is like a huge amount of impact.

00:33:41.207 --> 00:33:44.681
On screen at the moment we've been 
verifying that using Lighthouse,

00:33:44.681 --> 00:33:48.581
but you can also tell 
if individual requests,

00:33:48.581 --> 00:33:52.427
you know, were server pushed
using things like dev tools

00:33:52.427 --> 00:33:56.335
and using things like webpage tests
network waterfall view.

00:33:56.335 --> 00:33:58.097
Both are very, very handy.

00:33:58.386 --> 00:34:01.639
Now, we're on to 
our very last metric-- Hurray!

00:34:02.508 --> 00:34:05.266
Chloé didn't optimize
for First Input Delay,

00:34:05.266 --> 00:34:07.565
but I did want to very quickly cover it.

00:34:07.565 --> 00:34:09.294
Now, First Input Delay

00:34:09.294 --> 00:34:13.200
measures the time from when 
a user first interacts with a page,

00:34:13.200 --> 00:34:18.817
so that moment when they start 
to click on a button or tap some UI,

00:34:19.294 --> 00:34:21.388
some JavaScript powered control,

00:34:21.388 --> 00:34:23.033
to the time that the browser

00:34:23.033 --> 00:34:25.920
is actually able to respond
to that interaction.

00:34:26.368 --> 00:34:30.395
There are many things that cause
a poor First Input Delay.

00:34:30.395 --> 00:34:33.947
There can be long tasks
on the main thread,

00:34:33.947 --> 00:34:36.812
heavy JavaScript execution.

00:34:36.812 --> 00:34:39.353
Large JavaScript bundles can delay

00:34:39.353 --> 00:34:42.991
how soon script 
can be processed by the browser

00:34:42.991 --> 00:34:44.847
and it can have an impact here.

00:34:44.847 --> 00:34:47.527
And then, you have things 
like render blocking script.

00:34:47.527 --> 00:34:53.052
Now in general, I would strongly recommend
using Lighthouse and using dev tools

00:34:53.052 --> 00:34:56.501
because they do try to point out 
areas where you might have long tasks

00:34:56.501 --> 00:34:58.111
or heavy script execution.

00:34:58.111 --> 00:35:01.638
Very often the solution 
is to just break up this work,

00:35:01.638 --> 00:35:04.327
serve what the user needs
when they need it

00:35:04.327 --> 00:35:06.876
and try to look at opportunities

00:35:06.876 --> 00:35:10.652
for, you know, minimizing 
main thread work as much as possible.

00:35:11.177 --> 00:35:14.098
Sometimes people will contextualize this

00:35:14.098 --> 00:35:16.648
in terms of, you know, 
maybe shift some of that work,

00:35:16.648 --> 00:35:19.907
some of the logic work to a web worker.

00:35:19.907 --> 00:35:22.595
But regardless of the path
you want to take there,

00:35:23.783 --> 00:35:26.365
the end goal is essentially
just making sure

00:35:26.365 --> 00:35:28.235
that the main thread isn't busy

00:35:28.235 --> 00:35:30.318
and that user interactions
are not delayed.

00:35:30.802 --> 00:35:34.214
So, we're almost at the end
of our journey with Chloé.

00:35:34.843 --> 00:35:39.018
Here we can take a look 
at Chloe's overall Web Vitals in the lab.

00:35:39.018 --> 00:35:43.104
Thanks to their investments 
in performance and user experience,

00:35:43.104 --> 00:35:45.859
they were able to reduce 
their Cumulative Layout Shifts

00:35:45.859 --> 00:35:49.529
down to zero, 
and their LCP by almost half.

00:35:49.529 --> 00:35:52.428
This is mind-blowingly awesome.

00:35:52.428 --> 00:35:54.146
This is like really, really cool.

00:35:54.146 --> 00:35:58.404
As you've seen, all of this work
is kind of the culmination

00:35:58.404 --> 00:36:01.201
of a number of smaller optimizations

00:36:01.201 --> 00:36:05.161
that when added up, actually make 
a pretty significant impact

00:36:05.161 --> 00:36:06.736
to your end user experience.

00:36:06.736 --> 00:36:09.155
And we don't have 
to just look at data in the lab,

00:36:09.155 --> 00:36:10.785
we can look at the field as well.

00:36:10.785 --> 00:36:15.134
Here is Chrome user experience
report data for Chloé.

00:36:15.134 --> 00:36:16.470
And as we can see,

00:36:16.470 --> 00:36:19.621
our Core Web Vitals metrics
for LCP and CLS

00:36:19.621 --> 00:36:21.990
are trending in the right direction.

00:36:22.458 --> 00:36:27.516
CLS went from 0.85 down to 0
in the latest data set

00:36:27.516 --> 00:36:30.353
and this is all,
on the whole, tremendous work.

00:36:30.353 --> 00:36:31.769
It's really great to see,

00:36:31.769 --> 00:36:34.729
and I know that Chloé are happy to continue

00:36:34.729 --> 00:36:37.008
building on this work in the future as well.

00:36:37.605 --> 00:36:40.825
Now, if you're interested 
in building dashboards like this

00:36:40.825 --> 00:36:43.952
for your own team, 
measuring the Core Web Vitals,

00:36:43.952 --> 00:36:45.941
you might be interested in checking out

00:36:45.941 --> 00:36:48.066
the Chrome User Experience
Report Dashboard.

00:36:48.066 --> 00:36:51.788
This is a great solution 
that just allows you to drop in a URL

00:36:51.788 --> 00:36:55.652
and very quickly get access to field data

00:36:55.652 --> 00:36:58.195
and distributions 
for the different Core Web Vitals.

00:36:58.195 --> 00:37:00.270
It also summarizes the metrics.

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:03.393
So if you're trying 
to share around this report

00:37:03.393 --> 00:37:05.024
with other people on your team,

00:37:05.024 --> 00:37:08.162
they'll hopefully be able 
to also get some familiarity

00:37:08.162 --> 00:37:09.722
with the Core Web Vitals too.

00:37:09.722 --> 00:37:15.911
We also recently shipped a new Chrome
user experience report API, CrUX API.

00:37:15.911 --> 00:37:18.276
This is great for programmatically

00:37:18.276 --> 00:37:20.470
being able to build out
your own dashboards,

00:37:20.470 --> 00:37:22.985
very similar to what we were
just taking a look at.

00:37:22.985 --> 00:37:24.601
So check that out too.

00:37:24.601 --> 00:37:25.744
And that's it!

00:37:25.744 --> 00:37:28.965
I hope that you found this talk useful.

00:37:28.965 --> 00:37:31.734
Go and optimize your Web Vitals.

00:37:31.734 --> 00:37:34.564
There are plenty of docs over on <i>web.dev</i>

00:37:34.564 --> 00:37:38.661
that cover the methodology, the tools,
as well as the best practices

00:37:38.661 --> 00:37:42.025
that you can use 
to get fast and stay fast.

00:37:42.025 --> 00:37:43.526
My name is Addy Osmani.

00:37:43.526 --> 00:37:45.637
I hope this has been useful. 
Thank you.

00:37:45.637 --> 00:37:48.311
♪ (music) ♪

